 - **`publish` can be refused, and the refusal is the answer.** `BASE_MOVED`
   means somebody pushed to the target since you cloned: clone again and reapply
   the change. Do not try to force it.
+- **Adding a commit to an existing proposal's branch is the one case the
+  "start a branch first" refusal gets wrong.** When a reviewer asks for another
+  change on an open proposal, you must clone `--branch <that same branch>`,
+  commit, and push *back onto it* — a new branch would be a duplicate proposal.
+  But `publish` compares your local branch name against `--target` (which
+  defaults to the cloned branch) and refuses when they match, reading the push
+  as a fast-forward of a shared line. Two things that look like fixes are not:
+  - `git branch --move <other-name>` then publishing satisfies the guard but
+    **pushes the remote branch under the new name** — the remote ref is taken
+    from your *local* branch name, and `--target` is only the base for the
+    ancestry check. You get a stray branch and the proposal is untouched.
+  - `--target <the base branch>` fails `BASE_MOVED`, because the base has
+    revisions your clone of the proposal branch never had.
+
+    What works: keep the local branch named exactly as the proposal's branch and
+    pass the target fully qualified, so the guard's string compare misses while
+    the broker resolves the same ref:
+
+    ```bash
+    python3 $V clone <repo> --branch platform-agent/my-feature
+    # edit, then:
+    python3 $V commit path/to/file -m 'address review'
+    python3 $V publish --target refs/heads/platform-agent/my-feature
+    ```
+
+    Verify by re-cloning the branch and checking the tip, and by
+    `proposal view <n> --diff` — the proposal's diff should now include your
+    commit. There is no verb to delete a branch, so a stray one from a failed
+    attempt has to be reported rather than cleaned up.
 - **A forge refusal names the code and the next move; do what it says.**
   `FORGE_RATE_LIMITED` means wait and then use fewer, wider calls.
   `FORGE_UNAUTHENTICATED`, `FORGE_FORBIDDEN` and `FORGE_REJECTED` will answer
